WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.374
A useful OT alert describes a condition that matters and provides enough context for action.

00:00:06.374 --> 00:00:10.974
Examples include an unauthorized remote session, new communication across a boundary,

00:00:10.974 --> 00:00:15.575
a programming event outside maintenance, an unexpected device, repeated authentication failure,

00:00:15.575 --> 00:00:19.339
monitoring loss, or a change to a protected configuration.

00:00:19.339 --> 00:00:24.776
Triage should identify the source and destination assets, protocol, time, user or session,

00:00:24.776 --> 00:00:29.377
asset function, criticality, process state, related change records, and supporting telemetry.

00:00:29.377 --> 00:00:34.396
Analysts should determine whether the observation is approved, unexplained, a process fault,

00:00:34.396 --> 00:00:38.160
a security-control failure, or evidence of a cyber event.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.088
Operations and engineering teams are investigation partners.

00:00:41.088 --> 00:00:45.270
They can confirm maintenance, explain equipment behavior, identify process consequences,

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:48.198
and advise which containment actions are safe.

00:00:48.198 --> 00:00:53.216
A security analyst may recognize unusual network behavior while an operator recognizes

00:00:53.216 --> 00:00:57.399
that it coincides with a valve fault or production transition.

00:00:57.399 --> 00:00:59.908
Preserve evidence before it is overwritten.

00:00:59.908 --> 00:01:04.927
Record alert details, packet or log references, affected assets, timelines, analyst decisions,

00:01:04.927 --> 00:01:05.764
and communications.

00:01:05.764 --> 00:01:12.037
Protect evidence integrity and control access because OT telemetry may reveal sensitive architecture and operations.

00:01:12.037 --> 00:01:16.638
Escalation workflows should define severity, contacts, response authority, and expected time.

00:01:16.638 --> 00:01:19.147
High-consequence conditions may require immediate coordination,

00:01:19.147 --> 00:01:22.075
but automated isolation should be used cautiously.

00:01:22.075 --> 00:01:26.676
Disconnecting a system can remove operator visibility, redundancy, or required control.

00:01:26.676 --> 00:01:28.767
Close the loop after investigation.

00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:32.531
Document the disposition, update inventory or baselines, tune detection,

00:01:32.531 --> 00:01:36.713
correct the underlying control, and capture lessons for response plans.

00:01:36.713 --> 00:01:41.732
Alert quality improves when false positives and missed context are treated as

00:01:41.732 --> 00:01:44.660
engineering feedback rather than merely analyst workload.
